  "question": {
    "text": "The metal that has very low melting point and its periodic position is closer to a metalloid is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ga</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Se</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>In</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The expected order of melting points of&nbsp;group 13 elements is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>Al</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>Ga</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>In</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>Tl</mi></math>.</p><p>But due to structural&nbsp;changes, melting point instead of decreasing, increases from&nbsp;Ga&nbsp;to&nbsp;Tl&nbsp;and Ga&nbsp;has the lowest melting point. Hence,&nbsp;the actual order is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>Al</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>Tl</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>In</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>Ga</mi></math></p><p>Melting point</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>933</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ga</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>303</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>In</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>490</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p>Ga has low meting point and it is closer to metalloid in periodic table.</p></blockquote>"
  }
